## Why This Question Is Asked

This question evaluates practical knowledge of decoupled processing patterns, per-tenant isolation strategies, feature flag management, and secure configuration handling in Azure. It also assesses the candidate's ability to design a robust end-to-end workflow with appropriate rollback mechanisms.

## Key Concepts

- HTTP-triggered Azure Functions for API endpoints
- Azure Storage Queues for per-tenant message decoupling
- Cosmos DB partitioning using tenantId for data isolation
- Azure App Configuration for feature flags
- Azure Key Vault for secrets management
- CI/CD pipelines across dev, stage, and production environments
- Rollback strategies and poison message handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Function A: HTTP trigger - enqueue to per-tenant queue
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const { tenantId, payload } = req.body;

  if (!tenantId || !payload) {
    context.res = { status: 400, body: 'Missing tenantId or payload' };
    return;
  }

  const queueName = `tenant-${tenantId}-queue`;
  const queueClient = new QueueClient(connectionString, queueName);

  await queueClient.sendMessage(JSON.stringify({ tenantId, payload, timestamp: new Date() }));

  context.res = { status: 202, body: 'Message enqueued' };
};

// Function B: Queue trigger - write to Cosmos DB
module.exports = async function (context, msg) {
  const { tenantId, payload } = JSON.parse(msg.messageText);

  const featureFlags = await getFeatureFlag(`feature-flag-${tenantId}`);
  if (!featureFlags.writeEnabled) {
    context.log(`Writes disabled for tenant: ${tenantId}`);
    return;
  }

  const container = cosmosClient.database('tenant-db').container('events');
  await container.items.create({ id: context.invocationId, tenantId, payload });
};
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question assesses real-world Azure multi-tenant flag system design, evaluating understanding of latency optimization, tenant isolation, and change management in production environments.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags with targeting rules and environment support
- Low-latency read path with Redis caching and appropriate TTLs
- Strict per-tenant data isolation using key scoping (e.g., `flags:{tenantId}:{env}`)
- Change propagation from App Configuration to Redis via webhook triggers or event-driven updates
- Observability and auditing using Application Insights and OpenTelemetry
- Rollback/versioning strategies and experimentation/traffic splitting techniques

## Code Example

```javascript
async function getFlags(tenantId, env, userId) {
  const key = `flags:${tenantId}:${env}`;
  const cached = await redis.hGet(key, userId);
  if (cached) return JSON.parse(cached);

  const flags = await fetchFromAppConfig(tenantId, env);
  const userFlags = evaluateTargeting(flags, userId);
  await redis.hSet(key, userId, JSON.stringify(userFlags), { EX: 60 });
  return userFlags;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
The task tests basic serverless HTTP handling, per-tenant isolation, and simple rate-limiting logic using Azure Redis Cache. It touches deployment concerns (bindings, configuration) and error handling in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Functions HTTP trigger in JavaScript/TypeScript
- Redis INCR with TTL-based rate limiting
- Per-tenant isolation and validation
- Basic error handling and logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const tenantId = req.params.tenantId || req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
  if (!tenantId) return { status: 400, body: 'Missing tenantId' };
  // validate allowlist (omitted for brevity)
  const key = `rate:${tenantId}:count`;
  const count = await redis.incr(key);
  if (count === 1) await redis.expire(key, 1);
  if (count > 1) return { status: 429, headers: { 'Retry-After': '1' }, body: '{"error":"Rate limit"}' };
  return { status: 200, body: '{"message":"Hello, '+tenantId+'!"}' };
};
```

